Game Design Document - "ShooterX"

        Version 1.0.0

    Date 30 January 2026


Table of Contents

    1. Background
    2. Core Gameplay Loop
    3. Theme
    4. Levels
    5. Requirements
    6. Lessons Learned
    7. Change Log

    
1. Background 

    1.1     In thear 2120, a mysterious character with the alias “shooter”
    is mentally trapped inside a mind-chip. The chip, intended to enhance
    everyday life, malfunctions and forces shooter to believe he's living
    reality as a former cop. The truth is, he's bed-bound and fighting to
    escape a simulation. The corporate entity who designed the chip, Chart
    Up Corp, is responsible for the malicious chip-malfunction, with the
    goal of forcing advertisements throughout the simulation to embed
    subconscious future spending habits into its victims. Some inhabitants
    within the simulation are aware of the compromise of their brains, and
    within this digital world that intertwines with consciousness, they
    attempt to help shooter accept reality and to escape his fate.


    1.2     The player explores a dark cyberpunk-themed world looking for
    difficult to find pickups to include ammo, first-aid kits, and money to
    boost survival odds, and fights off administrators to escape the
    simulation.

    1.3     When the player dies, they are re-awakened in their home and lose
    their money. Chart Up Corp administrators, armed guards who
    hunt down shooter, aim to kill the player so that the perpetual death loop
    keeps them trapped within the simulation. The very long dream persists
    until the company deems the mind-chip marketing programming is
    complete, unless the player can end the simulation once and for all.


2. Core Gameplay Loop

    2.1     Explore the city to find pickups and cash.
    2.2     Play mini-games to earn cash.
    2.3     Fight off Chart Up Corp administrators.
    2.4     Visit locations to purchase supplies.
    2.5     Repeat until the player escapes the simulation or dies.

3. Theme 

    3.1     NES graphics with a cyberpunk look and feel. 
    Dark, gritty, and centralizes around a neon-lit city with a grand 
    casino—headquarters for Chart Up Corp. 


4. Levels 

    4.1     City

        The centralized location where shooter must explore, fight, and 
        learn how to escape his fate. 

    4.2     Bank
    
        The financial district where shooter can deposit and withdraw 
        virtual cash.

    4.3     Shooting Range

        A practice area where shooter can hone his shooting skills and 
        earn cash prizes.

        Three modes: Easy, Medium, Hard. Cash prizes commensurate with 
        level of difficulty completed.

    4.4     Casino
    
        Roulette and slots to win cash for buying supplies. 

    4.5     Pawn Shop
    
        To purchase ammo and body armor. 

    4.6     Hospital 

        To purchase first-aid kits. 

 
5. Requirements

    5.1     Levels must be designed to emulate NES graphics and colors.
    5.2     Player character must have walking and shooting animations.
    5.3     Enemies must have walking and shooting animations.
    5.4     A city level where the primary game takes place.
    5.5     A bank level where the player can deposit and withdraw cash.
    5.6     A shooting range level with three difficulty modes.
    5.7     A casino level with roulette and slot machines.
    5.8     A pawn shop level where the player can buy ammo and body armor
    5.9     A hospital level where the player can buy first-aid kits.
    5.10    Sound effects for walking, shooting, and item pickups.
    5.11    Background music for each level.
    5.12    A user interface displaying health, ammo, and cash.
    5.13    A save/load system to preserve player progress.
    5.14    An enemy AI system for Chart Up Corp administrators.
    5.15    A death and respawn system that resets player cash upon death.
    5.16    Entereable buildings for locations to grind for cash and items.
    5.17    A final level to fighthordes of administrators to escape
            the simulation.


6. Lessons Learned

    6.1     Underestimating NES Palette Limitations
            Solely using the NES pallette makes blending and
            smooth level design challenging. An alternative 
            approach is to use fewer colors.
    
